INT. FRONT PORCH - SUMMER EVENING

A wooden porch with a creaky swing, a pitcher of lemonade sweating on a side table, a glow of fireflies in the yard beyond.

GRANDPA ARCHIBALD "ARCHIE" TURNBULL (70s, suspenders, a straw hat) rocks in a wicker chair and whittles a small wooden bird. His granddaughter PENELOPE (10, scraped knees, a net) sits cross-legged on the boards. Her cousin BARNABY (8, a jar with holes punched in the lid) kneels at the porch rail.

ARCHIE
Easy with the net, Penny.

PENELOPE
I'm being easy.

ARCHIE
You're swinging it like a baseball bat.

PENELOPE
It's the only way to catch one.

BARNABY
I caught three already!

PENELOPE
You did not.

BARNABY
I did too.

PENELOPE
Show me.

Barnaby holds up the jar. It glows faintly. Penelope leans in, then leaps up, swings the net, and tumbles into the lemonade table. The pitcher wobbles. Archie reaches out and steadies it. Barnaby drops the jar, which rolls off the porch and into the grass. Penelope scrambles after it, net flailing. Barnaby follows. Archie sets down the whittling knife and chuckles.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
(from the yard)
It got out!

BARNABY
(from the yard)
Three got out!

ARCHIE
Let them go, children. They know the way home.

Penelope and Barnaby stand in the dark yard, surrounded by tiny flickering lights, their faces turned upward.

PENELOPE
(softly)
Wow.

BARNABY
(softly)
Yeah.

Archie picks up his whittling again. The swing creaks. The fireflies drift.
